/-!
# The push generator with a continue glider

A fan-out placed in front of `pushGenerator` (research note `N-15`): one control glider makes
the assembly emit the pushing salvo **and** a continue glider. Design `PSG1C` of the project's own
search (report `notes/research/reports/R-23-push-generator.md`, section 4), with the fan-out
moved 15 cells further back along the control lane (and the control glider 60 generations
earlier), so that the fan-out is separated from the generator's box by rows.

* `pushFan`: two blocked pairs of guns, period 30 (`pushFan_evolve_thirty`).
* `pushFan_phase1` (450 generations, three parts): with its control glider `pushFanControl`, the
  fan-out emits the control glider of `pushGenerator` and is left in the intermediate state
  `pushFanMid` (its own reaction is not over).
* `pushFan_phase2` (450 generations): `pushFanMid` finishes: the fan-out is back in its cycle and
  the continue glider `pushCont` is on its way; all cells stay in `x ≤ -92 ∨ y ≤ -130`
  (certified by `life_corner`).
* `incrementContinue_evolve`: the whole assembly `pushGenerator ∪ pushFan` with the control
  glider evolves in 2100 generations into itself, the pushing salvo and the continue glider
  `pushContEnd`.

The proof composes the certificates: phase 1 beside the resting generator; then the first three
parts of `pushGenerator_reaction` beside phase 2 of the fan-out; then the remaining eight parts
beside the resting fan-out and the continue glider in flight.

Not covered: the same with the register block (the footprint of `push3_reaction` bounds rows only).
-/

